checkpoints from Caspian:
[p] pass, [w] warn, [f] fail, [n/a] not applicable.
1. dataset & audio/text quality
[ ] source & licensing: source/license matches the claim and strictly allows commercial challenge use, reuse, and redistribution.
[ ] raw assets only: uploaded files must be raw data, generator scripts, or source assets (e.g., raw audio wavs, unparsed text)—not pre-baked train/test splits.
[ ] speech/text domain fit: for speech ai (asr/tts) or code-switching/text data, annotations and labels must be clean, meaningful, and represent real-world technical/professional usage, not arbitrary patterns.
[ ] synthetic sanity check: if synthetic data is used, the generation script must be provided. the data must model a plausible process, not just a toy setup with basic labels pasted on.
2. challenge design & evaluation
[ ] novelty: nearest-neighbor benchmark/task is identified and the challenge is not a near-duplicate of the source dataset's canonical task.
[ ] real-world ml value: the problem must test actual modeling or generalization skills, not just a contrived "challenge for challenge's sake."
[ ] fair split & ood testing: splits must test realistic generalization. out-of-distribution (ood) test sets are only valid if the target remains learnable from the training signal or domain structure, not impossible guessing.
[ ] metric & headroom: the chosen metric must fit the domain perfectly. the baseline score must leave at least ~30% improvement headroom to separate weak, decent, and elite submissions.
3. robust grader & exception handling
[ ] input validation: grader must actively reject malformed submissions (wrong columns, missing rows, duplicate ids, nan/inf, invalid ranges) instead of scoring them.
[ ] no internal leaks: grader errors must never leak private test labels, answer keys, split logic, or internal scoring weights.
[ ] agent-code safeguards: check the submission pipelines carefully. ensure there are no sketchy try...except fallback mechanisms (like dropping deep learning models entirely models = [] to rely on classical interpolation hacks) designed to bypass hidden test set fluctuations or error logs.
4. leakage & shortcuts
[ ] feature isolation: public features must not reveal targets through metadata, ids, row order, file lengths, or hidden text artifacts.
[ ] no rule leaks: prompts and data must not expose answer logic, generator rules, or hidden scoring tricks.
[ ] anti-regex/anti-lookup: the task cannot be solvable by simple regex, lookup tables, metadata matching, or template inversion. it must require actual machine learning.
